WEBVTT

00:00:00.550 --> 00:00:02.345
Lesson 7.1.

00:00:02.345 --> 00:00:04.300
On-chain data foundations.

00:00:04.300 --> 00:00:06.530
By the end of this lesson, you'll be able

00:00:06.530 --> 00:00:09.025
to avoid the classic misreads of on-chain data.

00:00:10.325 --> 00:00:11.615
Here's the plan.

00:00:11.615 --> 00:00:13.300
First, the key ideas.

00:00:13.300 --> 00:00:16.015
Then, a worked example, step by step.

00:00:16.015 --> 00:00:17.540
Then, your checklist.

00:00:17.540 --> 00:00:19.702
And finally, a three-question quiz.

00:00:21.002 --> 00:00:24.255
First, Address ≠ person: one person can have

00:00:24.255 --> 00:00:28.387
many addresses; one exchange address serves millions.

00:00:28.387 --> 00:00:30.832
Next, Transfer ≠ trade: moving

00:00:30.832 --> 00:00:33.577
tokens isn't buying or selling.

00:00:33.577 --> 00:00:36.755
Then, Labels are probabilistic: providers guess who

00:00:36.755 --> 00:00:38.827
owns what; they can be wrong.

00:00:38.827 --> 00:00:41.338
Also, Active addresses can be inflated

00:00:41.338 --> 00:00:43.377
by bots or airdrop farming.

00:00:43.377 --> 00:00:45.989
And finally, Data provenance: know chain

00:00:45.989 --> 00:00:48.282
coverage, indexing method and delay.

00:00:49.582 --> 00:00:52.097
Let's work an example, step by step.

00:00:52.097 --> 00:00:55.475
"Whale moves $100M of BTC!" It's an exchange

00:00:55.475 --> 00:00:59.304
shuffling between its own cold and hot wallets.

00:00:59.304 --> 00:01:00.799
No trade happened.

00:01:00.799 --> 00:01:04.109
Check labels, destination and history before reacting.

00:01:05.409 --> 00:01:06.869
Here's your checklist.

00:01:06.869 --> 00:01:08.404
Do it now, for real.

00:01:08.404 --> 00:01:10.063
First, I check what an address

00:01:10.063 --> 00:01:11.969
is before interpreting a move.

00:01:11.969 --> 00:01:15.409
And finally, I note each metric's source and definition.

00:01:16.709 --> 00:01:17.919
Question 1.

00:01:17.919 --> 00:01:19.594
Does an address equal a person?

00:01:24.014 --> 00:01:25.189
The answer: No.

00:01:26.489 --> 00:01:27.734
Question 2.

00:01:27.734 --> 00:01:29.359
Is a large transfer a sale?

00:01:33.779 --> 00:01:35.904
The answer: Not necessarily.

00:01:35.904 --> 00:01:37.839
It may be internal or custodial.

00:01:39.139 --> 00:01:40.409
Question 3.

00:01:40.409 --> 00:01:42.454
Why can active-address counts mislead?

00:01:46.874 --> 00:01:49.329
The answer: Bots and farming inflate them.

00:01:50.629 --> 00:01:51.874
Let's recap.

00:01:51.874 --> 00:01:56.334
Address ≠ person: one person can have many addresses.

00:01:56.334 --> 00:02:00.914
Transfer ≠ trade: moving tokens isn't buying or selling.

00:02:00.914 --> 00:02:04.864
Labels are probabilistic: providers guess who owns what.

00:02:04.864 --> 00:02:06.960
Active addresses can be inflated

00:02:06.960 --> 00:02:08.674
by bots or airdrop farming.

00:02:09.974 --> 00:02:12.489
Do the checklist now, before you move on.

00:02:12.489 --> 00:02:16.104
Next up, Lesson 7.2: Block explorer mastery.
